Six examples that predate the checkpointing protocol.

They call an API the library no longer has -- set_elim_mode(), REVERSE_ELIM
and reverse_elimination() -- so none of them compiles, and none has compiled
for a long time.  The hand-maintained list in the old test/Makefile named
eleven of the seventeen directories and simply left these six out, which is
why nobody noticed.

They are frozen here rather than deleted because they are the only surviving
record of what the pre-checkpoint surface looked like, and because
minmax/main.cpp in particular exercises seven different min/max shapes that
nothing else in the tree covers -- worth porting rather than rewriting.

Kept, per directory:  main.cpp only.

Dropped:  the one-line Makefile and the SKIP marker each of them carried.
Both were about being discovered by examples/Makefile, and outside examples/
there is nothing to be discovered by.  Their original pre-build-tree
Makefiles are gone: they were overwritten before the tree was under version
control, which the first commit's message records.  ctors/ is the exception
and its original is in that commit.

TO PORT ONE BACK.  Move the directory into examples/, add a Makefile of

    RUNARGS = <whatever it needs>
    include ../common.mk

and replace the three dead calls.  There is no list to add it to:
examples/Makefile discovers whatever is there.  fdcheck already covers all
forty-eight elementary functions against central differences, so a ported
example is worth having for what it demonstrates, not for what it checks.

This follows Maxwell, which froze its own seven pre-checkpoint examples in
backup_20260831/ for the same reason.
